  "title": "How to Buy Anthropic Stock: IPO Status and Rules",
  "metaDescription": "Learn how to buy Anthropic stock, what the filed Form S-1 shows, how initial public offering allocations work, and what risks pre-IPO offerings carry.",
  "h1": "How to Buy Anthropic Stock",
  "cardBlurb": "Anthropic filed Form S-1 with the SEC to list on Nasdaq under ticker ANT, but shares cannot be bought on any public stock exchange today.",
  "introText": "You cannot buy Anthropic stock today. No Anthropic shares trade on any public stock exchange.\n\nAnthropic, PBC filed its Form S-1 registration statement with the Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) on October 26, 2026. The company proposed to list its Class A common stock on the Nasdaq Global Select Market under the ticker symbol ANT. The initial filing left both the estimated share price range and the number of shares blank. Until an amended registration statement appears and the stock lists on Nasdaq, everyday investors cannot buy shares through standard market orders.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "Can You Buy Anthropic Stock Today?",
      "body": "No shares of Anthropic trade on any public stock exchange today. Anthropic filed a Form S-1 registration statement with the Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) on October 26, 2026, but the filing does not make shares immediately available for purchase. The initial public offering (IPO) process requires multiple regulatory steps before any member of the public can trade shares.\n\nInvestors can understand the path to public trading by tracking four distinct stages:\n\n1. Current status: Anthropic Class A common stock has no public market. You cannot log into a brokerage account and buy shares today because no exchange lists the company.\n\n2. Pricing stage: Anthropic and its underwriters must file an amended Form S-1 that fills in the estimated initial public offering price range and the planned number of shares. Underwriters will then conduct an investor roadshow to determine final pricing.\n\n3. Listing day: Once the registration statement becomes effective, the underwriters price the offering and the stock opens for public trading. At that point, investors can place a standard market order or limit order to buy ANT shares through any brokerage account.\n\n4. Transactions to ignore: Investors should avoid private pre-IPO offers. These unregistered deals carry steep risks, high costs, and potential fraud.\n\nUntil listing day arrives, retail market orders cannot execute."
    },
    {
      "heading": "What the S-1 Says",
      "body": "The preliminary prospectus filed with the Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) provides initial disclosures about the corporate structure, underwriters, and reported financial results. Anthropic is incorporated as a public benefit corporation under Delaware law. The filing states that prior to this offering, there has been no public market for the company's Class A common stock. All reported figures represent the company's own accounting disclosures rather than an independent market valuation.\n\n| Item | What the filing shows |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| Filing Date | October 26, 2026 (SEC EDGAR accession 0001628280-26-067168) |\n| Proposed Exchange and Ticker | Nasdaq Global Select Market under the symbol ANT |\n| Offering Price Range | Blank in initial Form S-1 filing |\n| Number of Shares Offered | Blank in initial Form S-1 filing |\n| Lead Underwriting Representatives | Goldman Sachs & Co. LLC and Morgan Stanley & Co. LLC |\n| Underwriting Syndicate Size | 22 listed underwriters |\n| Overallotment Option | 30-day option for underwriters to purchase additional shares |\n| Full Year 2024 Revenue | $386 million |\n| Full Year 2025 Revenue | $4,585 million |\n| Six Months Ended June 30, 2025 Revenue | $1,163 million |\n| Six Months Ended June 30, 2026 Revenue | $16,286 million |\n| Six Months Ended June 30, 2025 Operating Loss | $(2,135) million |\n| Six Months Ended June 30, 2026 Operating Loss | $(693) million |\n| Six Months Ended June 30, 2025 Net Loss | $(12,969) million |\n| Six Months Ended June 30, 2026 Net Loss | $(80,748) million |\n| Fair Value Adjustment (Six Months Ended June 30, 2026) | $(72,388) million change in fair value of convertible notes, derivative liabilities, and SAFEs |\n\nAt ModernWallet, in our review of the filed [Form S-1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2130357/000162828026067168/anthropicpbc-sx1.htm), we searched the full text for a directed share program, a reserved-share program, and any retail allocation, and found none; the filing does say that the representatives may agree to allocate a number of shares of Class A common stock to underwriters for sale to their online brokerage account holders.\n\nThe financial disclosures show that the company generated $16,286 million in revenue during the first six months of 2026, compared to $1,163 million during the first six months of 2025. Operating loss narrowed from $2,135 million to $693 million across those same periods. Net loss widened to $80,748 million for the six months ended June 30, 2026, driven by a non-cash charge of $72,388 million from the change in fair value of convertible notes, derivative liabilities, and simple agreements for future equity (SAFEs). The Form S-1 explicitly notes that the results for the six-month period are not indicative of results for the full financial year."
    },
    {
      "heading": "How Retail Investors Get IPO Shares",
      "body": "Retail access to initial public offering shares before exchange trading begins is strictly limited by industry practice and underwriting distribution rules. The [SEC investor bulletin on IPOs](https://www.sec.gov/files/ipo-investorbulletin.pdf) explains that underwriters distribute the majority of initial public offering shares to institutional accounts and high-net-worth clients first. Because of this distribution model, most individual investors can only purchase shares once the stock begins trading on the secondary market, which frequently opens at a price above the original initial public offering price.\n\nUnderwriting distributions are regulated under [FINRA Rule 5131](https://www.finra.org/rules-guidance/rulebooks/finra-rules/5131), which establishes guidelines for new issue allocations and prohibits abusive allocation practices. Anthropic's Form S-1 registration statement notes that the lead representatives may allocate shares to underwriters for distribution to their online brokerage account holders. The filing also states that the underwriters do not expect sales to discretionary accounts to exceed five percent of the total shares offered. To learn more about how public offerings function, review our guide on [what is an IPO](/guides/what-is-an-ipo/).\n\nIf you want to participate in an offering before public trading starts, you must ask your individual brokerage firm whether it offers initial public offering access and what eligibility criteria it enforces. Brokerages frequently establish balance thresholds, account minimums, or trading history requirements before permitting clients to request initial public offering allocations. The prospectus contains no guarantee that retail account holders will receive any shares."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Pre-IPO Offers and Scams",
      "body": "Unsolicited opportunities to purchase private shares before an initial public offering present significant financial hazards. According to the [Investor.gov pre-IPO alert](https://www.investor.gov/additional-resources/news-alerts/alerts-bulletins/investor-alert-pre-ipo-investment-scams-updated) issued on June 7, 2024, pre-IPO investing carries substantial risk, including the potential loss of your entire investment principal. Private pre-IPO offerings are not registered with the Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC), and offerings marketed broadly to the general public may be illegal.\n\nInvestors face several structural traps when participating in unregistered private share sales:\n\n- No public listing: The company may delay its plans indefinitely or cancel its public offering altogether.\n- Liquidity lock: A secondary trading market may never develop, leaving buyers unable to resell or transfer their holdings.\n- Complete capital loss: The issuer may fail, rendering unregistered private contracts worthless.\n\nThe alert highlights multiple warning signs associated with private stock solicitations. Red flags include unregistered individuals or firms offering private equity deals, aggressive sales tactics such as unsolicited cold calls, marketing pushes on social media channels, and high-pressure claims centered around popular emerging technologies. Before transferring money to any entity promising access to private shares, verify the registration status of the seller using the search database on [Investor.gov](https://www.investor.gov/additional-resources/news-alerts/alerts-bulletins/investor-alert-pre-ipo-investment-scams-updated)."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Lock-Up and What Happens After Listing",
      "body": "Existing shareholders, founders, and employees cannot immediately dump all of their stock on the first day of trading. The Form S-1 registration statement establishes a 180-day lock-up and market standoff period for company insiders and certain stockholders, along with an extended 365-day lock-up period for specified holders. The filing notes that the underwriting representatives retain discretionary authority to release lock-up securities in whole or in part prior to expiration.\n\nThe [SEC lock-up guide](https://www.sec.gov/fast-answers/answerslockuphtm.html) notes that lock-up agreements restrict company insiders from selling their shares for a designated window of time following a public listing. These contracts prevent an immediate flood of insider-owned shares into the market while the stock establishes initial trading patterns. Once these contractual restrictions expire or underwriters grant releases, substantial blocks of previously restricted shares can enter public circulation and reach the market."
    },
    {
      "heading": "What to Do While You Wait",
      "body": "Investors preparing for a future public listing can take practical steps to educate themselves and manage financial risk. First-time equity buyers can review core fundamentals in our guide on [how to start investing](/guides/how-to-start-investing/).\n\n- Read the primary regulatory filing: Review the complete text and operational risk factors in the [Anthropic Form S-1](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2130357/000162828026067168/anthropicpbc-sx1.htm) on the SEC EDGAR system.\n- Monitor future amendments: Watch the SEC filing feed for an amended Form S-1 that populates the anticipated share price range and the exact number of Class A shares offered.\n- Restrict single-stock risk: Maintain disciplined asset allocation limits to avoid overexposing your savings to a single company, as detailed in our guide on [concentrated stock position risk](/guides/concentrated-stock-position-risk/).\n- Examine historical precedents: Read our overview of [how to buy SpaceX stock](/guides/how-to-buy-spacex-stock/), which examines how trading unfolded when SpaceX listed its shares on Nasdaq as SPCX on June 12, 2026.\n\nPreparing your brokerage account and understanding filing data ensures you make grounded choices rather than chasing volatile trading swings."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Our Takeaway",
      "body": "You cannot buy Anthropic stock today because no shares trade on any public stock exchange. The realistic avenues for acquiring shares later are an initial public offering allocation through an online broker that receives stock from the underwriting syndicate or placing an order for ANT on Nasdaq after public trading commences. The Form S-1 filing notes that underwriters may allocate shares to their online brokerage account holders, but it promises retail investors nothing.\n\nInvestors should reject private pre-IPO solicitations, which the Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) warns carry severe hazards of total capital loss and fraud. Wait for the updated Form S-1 filing to disclose the proposed share count and price range before making portfolio decisions, and keep single-stock allocations sized defensively. This assessment is based on our review of the Form S-1 and official regulatory releases, not a product test. Our takeaway will update if an amended registration statement introduces a formal retail allocation or alters the proposed listing structure."
    }
